  STRATEGY AND MONSTER INFO

--Note first of all that you only get one smart bomb per life, and if 
you don't use it, the bombs don't add up.  The point bears repeating!
--The 60-point monsters are faster than the 40-pointers, who are faster 
than the 20-pointers.
--Monsters can't step over each other to get to you.
--In addition, the 60-point monsters, if they are directly to your left 
or right in open space as opposed to a hallway, zigzag up and down, 
making them quite hard to hit.  Other monsters move diagonally(i.e. 
left, then up, then left, then up, etc.) until horizontally aligned with 
you.
--A lot of times you'll want to run into a wall to stand still and just 
wait for monsters to come at you, since running back and forth only 
makes you more likely to touch a monster, even if you do it quickly, and 
it's tiring, too.  You can't run into a wall right next to you--you have 
to get a running start.  So there are only special locations where you 
can stop and shoot.  To get by certain areas there may be nothing else 
you can do besides wait for monsters to come out more slowly.
--If you don't remember whether you have a key, look to see if you're 
carrying it.  It looks more like a sash than a key.  Also note you can 
only hold one key at once.
--Losing a guy can really stink because the game pushes you to the left 
to one of a handful of pre-specified locations--although if you are 
going back to retrieve a key anyway, it is actually a plus.
--You always have more than enough time to complete a level, even if you 
want to pick up all treasures, but it's up to you if you have enough 
patience.  Many times you just have to wait for monsters to stop 
swarming a certain area.
--Monsters follow you in a diagonal path in an open area, although 
sometimes when they are walking through more mazy parts they will go in 
the wrong direction.
--There are horizontal niches in mazes which are nice to use--you can 
blast monsters as they go past.  An example is to the side of the 
vertical corridor just before the wide open space in level two.
--If you are in open space, it is tough to time your shot so that you 
nail a sixty-pointer.  The best way to do things may be to run into a 
wall and wait for one to get close, because if you are moving around, 
things get tough to calculate.  Your shot to one side must vanish before 
you can shoot again that way.
--The vapor trail from your shot may touch a monster, but it won't kill 
it.  Be alert!
--This is a bit tricky, but if you shoot just as a monster bears down on 
you from above, you may nail it.  A good last resort.

